# engineering.local (monorepo root)

Primary model:

- Monorepo root is a control plane for shared docs, CI, ontology, and governance.
- Package/app members define language-specific stack contracts inside their own folders.

Executable contract surface:

- root `policy/engineering-lane.json` declares the monorepo control-plane posture, catalog/list commands, and scanner-visible root disciplines
- root `docs/engineering.local.md` explains monorepo control-plane deltas and selected cross-language disciplines
- package/app `policy/engineering-lane.json` declares the upstream `engineering-core` lane reference and catalog command when one exists
- package/app `docs/engineering.local.md` records local overrides

Upstream catalog retrieval:

```bash
uv tool -n run --from ~/ai-society/core/engineering-core engineering-core catalog --pretty
uv tool -n run --from ~/ai-society/core/engineering-core engineering-core list-disciplines
uv tool -n run --from ~/ai-society/core/engineering-core engineering-core list-templates
```

Common root disciplines:

- `validation`, `testing`, `documentation`, `dependency-governance`
- add `local-first-data`, `security-privacy`, `observability`, `design-system`, `accessibility`, `specification-and-dsls`, or `engineering-reasoning` when the monorepo root owns those concerns.
- add `build-graph-acceleration` only for measured build/test/package-task graph acceleration work involving Buck2, Bazel, Pants, Nx/Turborepo, remote cache, or remote execution; native package/app commands remain canonical until repo-local acceptance says otherwise.
- for Rust members, use `rust-build-graph` only as a conditional addendum after measured Rust build/test bottlenecks; Cargo remains canonical and Buck2/Bazel are optional acceleration overlays, not default requirements.

Practical rule:

- Use root commands for monorepo-wide validation.
- Use package/app local checks for language-specific validation.
- Use each package/app `policy/engineering-lane.json` as the source of truth for the declared upstream lane command; root docs should not hardcode package lane commands.
